{
  "definition": "A variance ratio compares the variance of a q-bar return against q times the variance of a one-bar return. Above 1 the moves reinforce each other; below 1 they cancel; 1.0 is a random walk. It is measured on each market's own consecutive bars — including Bitcoin's weekends — because a one-bar return means that market's own bar. The z is Lo-MacKinlay's heteroskedasticity-robust statistic: under 2 in absolute value, the market is not distinguishable from a random walk at that horizon, whatever the ratio says. Correlations are the opposite case and use only dates all six markets traded.",
